          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em relation
            is the extension of a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate"} predicate
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+value"} truth value
            may depend on some
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/true"} true
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target"} target
            is the set of truth values.
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %h3#DefinitionGeneralCase General case
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              Given a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/family+of+sets"} family
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo :
                        %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A_i)_{i: I}
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
              %strong relation
              on that family is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo :
                          %mi I
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{i: I} A_i
            %p
              Equivalently, this is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
              from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo :
                        %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{i: I} A_i
              to the set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} TV
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \TV
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+values"} truth values
              (because
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} TV
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \TV
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
              in
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            %p
              Also equivalently, this is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
              in/
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobject
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo ↪
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi i
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    R \hookrightarrow \prod_{i \colon I} A_i
                    \\,.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              the 2-element set (a
              = succeed ")" do
                %em binary relation
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondence"} correspondence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi R
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↙
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi A
                              %mn 1
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi A
                              %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp;&amp; R
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow
                    \\\
                    A_1 &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; A_1
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              Hence relations are precisely the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28-1%29-truncated"} (-1)-truncated
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondences"} correspondences
            %p
              This identification induces a natural notion of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
              of relations from the composition in the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+correspondences"} category of correspondences
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#The2PosetOfBinaryRelations"} The 2-poset of binary relations
            %p
              Formulated this way, the notion of relation generalizes to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
              other than
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
              and to
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
              %em
                %a{:href => "#Generalization"} Generalizations
              below.
          %h3#special_cases Special cases
          %p
            A
            %strong nullary relation
            is a relation on the empty family of sets. This is the same as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+value"} truth value
          %p
            A
            %strong
              unary relation on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a relation on the singleton family
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            A
            %strong
              binary relation on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is a relation on the family
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,B)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \times B
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                relation from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rel"} Rel
            described below, or sometimes called a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong heterogenous relation
          %p
            A
            %strong
              binary relation on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a relation on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,A)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to itself. This is sometimes called a
            %strong homogenous relation
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                relation on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %strong endorelation
            with its set implicit as a property if not explicitly mentioned.
          %p
            An
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              \-ary relation
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a relation on a family of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            copies of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^n
          %p
            For a binary relation, one often uses a symbol such as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
            and writes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \sim b
            instead of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo ∼
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,b) \in \sim
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mi R
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a R b
            instead of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,b) \in R
          %h3#internal_and_external_relations Internal and external relations
          %p
            In foundations of mathematics with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sort"} sort
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            and a separate sort of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first-order logic
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            = succeed "," do
              %strong internal relation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/family+of+sets"} family of sets
            %strong external relation
            is simply a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
            of a family of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i
            inside of a family of sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_i
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi n
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi P
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} prop
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma, x_0 \in A_0, x_1 \in A_1, x_2 \in A_2, \ldots, x_n \in A_n \vdash P \; \mathrm{prop}
          %p
            In any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unsorted+set+theory"} unsorted set theory
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} true
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} true
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} true
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi n
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} true
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi P
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} prop
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma, x_0, A_0, x_0 \in A_0 \; \mathrm{true}, x_1, A_1, x_1 \in A_1 \; \mathrm{true}, x_2, A_2, x_2 \in A_2 \; \mathrm{true}, \ldots, x_n, A_n, x_n \in A_n \; \mathrm{true} \vdash P \; \mathrm{prop}
          %p
            It is in this second sense of external relation that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic+with+equality"} first-order logic with equality
            is an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inequality"} inequality
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tight+apartness+relation"} tight apartness relation
            in a first-order theory with equality presented in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsingletons"} subsingletons
            or the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            themselves, there is only one notion of relation, the internal notion given above.
          %h2#morphisms Morphisms
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            are each sets equipped with a relation, then what makes a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: A \to B
            a
            %em morphism
            of sets so equipped?
          %p
            There are really two ways to do this, shown below. (We will write these as if each set is equipped with a binary relation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∼
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              %strong preserves
              the relation if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi y
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \sim y \;\Rightarrow\; f(x) \sim f(y)
              always;
            %li
              %em f
              %strong reflects
              the relation if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi y
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⇐
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \sim y \;\Leftarrow\; f(x) \sim f(y)
              always.
          %p
            Now, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            of relation-equipped sets should do both. What about a mere morphism?
          %p
            In general, it's more natural to require only preservation; these are the morphisms you get if you consider a set with a relation as a models of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} finite-limit theory
            or a simply
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+graph"} directed graph
          %p
            But in some contexts, particularly when dealing only with
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreflexive+relation"} irreflexive relation
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-order"} well-order
          %h2#binary_relations Binary relations
          %p Binary relations are especially widely used.
          %h3#kinds_of_binary_relations Kinds of binary relations
          %p
            Special kinds of relations from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            include:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+relations"}> functional relations
              ,
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entire+relations"}> entire relations
              ,
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/difunctional+relation"} difunctional relations
            %li
              %span.newWikiWord>
                one-to-one relation
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/one-to-one+relation"} ?
              s,
            %li
              %span.newWikiWord>
                onto relation
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/onto+relation"} ?
              s.
          %p Combinations of the above properties of binary relations produce:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"}> functions
              ,
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+functions"}> partial functions
              ,
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injections"}> injections
              ,
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjections"}> surjections
              ,
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijections"}> bijections
              \.
          %p
            Special kinds of binary relations on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            (so from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to itself) additionally include:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+relation"} reflexive
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreflexive+relation"} irreflexive
              relations;
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+relation"}> symmetric
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antisymmetric+relation"} antisymmetric
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymmetric+relation"} asymmetric
              relations;
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transitive+relations"} transitive relations
              and
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comparisons"} comparisons
            %li
              left and right
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/euclidean+relations"} euclidean relations
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+relation"} total
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+relation"} connected
              relations;
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+relation"} extensional
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-founded+relation"} well-founded
              relations.
          %p Combinations of the above properties of binary relations produce:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relations"}> equivalence relations
              ,
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+equivalence+relations"}> partial equivalence relations
              ,
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/apartness+relations"}> apartness relations
              ,
            %li
              the various kinds of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orders"} orders
              (which see).
          %h3#structure_preserving_relations Structure preserving relations
          %p
            Given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+sets"} pointed sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A, a)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (B, b)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is said to preserve the point if
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(a, b)
          %p
            Given sets with endofunctions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A, f_A)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (B, f_B)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is said to preserve the endofunction if for every
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in B
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(a, b)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(f(a), f(b))
          %p
            Given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magmas"} magmas
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A, \mu_A)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (B, \mu_b)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is said to preserve the binary operation if for every
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in B
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \in B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(a, b)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo ,
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(c, d)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(\mu_A(a, c), \mu_B(b, d))
          %p And so forth.
          %h3#properties Properties
          %p
            Every binary endorelation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            has an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim_R
            generated by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          %h3#binary_relations_as_a_coalgebra Binary relations as a coalgebra
          %p
            By
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/currying"} currying
            the binary relation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R: \mathcal{P}(X \times X) \cong X \times X \rightarrow \Omega
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coalgebra+for+an+endofunctor"} coalgebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi θ
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta: X \rightarrow (X \rightarrow \Omega) \cong X \rightarrow \mathcal{P}(X)
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/powerset"} powerset
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endofunctor"} endofunctor
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
          %h3#The2PosetOfBinaryRelations
            The
            = succeed "-poset" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %p
            Binary relations form a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} category
            (in fact a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-poset"} poset
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rel"} Rel
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/allegory"} allegory
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            are the binary relations on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-morphism"} 2-morphism
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            (both from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            implies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            (that is, when
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,b) \in R
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,b) \in S
          %p
            The interesting definition is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
          #CompositionOfRelations.num_defn
            %h6#defintion Defintion
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              is a relation from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              is a relation from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %em composite relation
              – written
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \circ R
              or
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo ;
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R;S
              – from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is defined as follows:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi R
                    %mo ;
                    %mi S
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⇔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi b
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi R
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ∧
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi S
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,c) \in R;S \;\Leftrightarrow\; \exists b: B,\; (a,b) \in R \;\wedge\; (b,c) \in S.
            %p
              The identity morphism is given by
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
              operation of relation from def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CompositionOfRelations"}
              is induced by the composition of the underlying
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondences"} correspondences
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28-1%29-truncated"} (-1)-truncation
          %p
            The special properties of the kinds of binary relations listed earlier can all be described in terms internal to
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rel"} Rel
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/allegory"} allegory
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-object"} hom-poset
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bottom"} bottom
            elements, and linear relations require this. Comparisons require the hom-posets to have finite
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/union"} unions
          %p
            As a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            may be seen as a functional, entire relation, so the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            of sets and functions is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategory"} subcategory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rel"} Rel
            (in fact a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/replete+subcategory"} replete
            and locally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full
            sub-
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            \-category).
          %h3#endorel The quasitopos of endorelations
          %p
            Endorelations on sets are the objects of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasitopos"} quasitopos
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi EndoRel
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} EndoRel
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Bin
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bin
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflective subcategory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quiv"} Quiv
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+presheaves"} presheaf topos
            of quivers and its morphisms are quiver morphisms. Endorelations are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separated+presheaf"} separated presheaves
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation#in_topos_theory"} double negation topology
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Quiv
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Quiv
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflector
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Quiv
                  %mo →
                  %mi EndoRel
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Quiv \to EndoRel
            collapses parallel arcs together. Such quivers might also be called
            %strong singular
            or
            %strong simple
            though sometimes “simple” also means “no loops”.
          %h4#relation_closures_as_reflective_subcategories_of_
            Relation closures as reflective subcategories of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi EndoRel
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} EndoRel
          %p
            All of the sub-types of endorelations with positive conditions (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+relation"}> reflexive
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+relation"} symmetric
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transitive+relations"} transitive
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/euclidean+relations"} euclidean
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+closure"} closure
            that can produce one from an arbitrary relation. Such a closure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completion"} completes
            a relation by adding the least number of arcs such that the conditions are satisfied. Within
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi EndoRel
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} EndoRel
            these closures are reflectors that produce reflective subcategories. For example the
            %strong symmetric closure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sym
                  %mo :
                  %mi EndoRel
                  %mo →
                  %mi EndoSym
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sym: EndoRel \to EndoSym
            will (possibly) enlarge a quiver that contains any arc
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v_a \to v_b
            to one that also contains
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mi b
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v_b \to v_a
            %strong transitive and reflexive closure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi transRef
                  %mo :
                  %mi EndoRel
                  %mo →
                  %mi EndoTransRef
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} transRef: EndoRel \to EndoTransRef
            produces a category which is isomorphic to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorder"} PreOrd
            though its objects are the underlying quivers of the preorders which are the objects of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi PreOrd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PreOrd
          %p
            In addition to being reflective, the categories from the
            = succeed "," do
              %em symmetric
            = succeed "," do
              %em reflexive
            %em symmetric and reflexive
            closures are also quasitoposes that can be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasitopos#exampsep"} directly defined
            through double negation separation. The
            %em symmetric and reflexive
            closure (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+simple+graphs"}> SimpGph
            ) is also a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasitopos"} Grothendieck quasitopos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi PreOrd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PreOrd
            is not a quasitopos because it is not a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular category
          %h2#ternary_relations Ternary relations
          %p
            Apart from binary relations, there are important ternary relations used in mathematics, which for the most part are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/families"} families
            of binary relations indexed by a third set:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniformity"}> uniformity
              , where the index set is the set of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entourages"} entourages
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/premetric"}> premetric
              , where the index set is the set of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
          %h2#Generalization Generalisation
          %p
            Most of the preceding makes sense in any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            with enough
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+relation"} internal relations
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/congruences"} congruences
            in the case of internal equivalence relations.
          %p
            Probably the trickiest bit is the definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            of binary relations, so not every category with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+products"} finite products
            has an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/allegory"} allegory
            of relations. In fact, in a certain precise sense, a category has an allegory of relations if and only if it is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rel"} Rel
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph"} graph
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+relation"} logical relation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+relation"} quantum relation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/corelation"} corelation
          %div
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th
                    type of
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
                  %th
